\title{Drag on smooth circular cylinders towed through a water tank}
\author{\todo[inline]{Authors and affiliations}}
\date{\today}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We report mean drag measurements on three smooth circular cylinders, of
diameter \result[DOneIn], \result[DOnePointFiveIn], and
\result[DTwoIn]~\si{mm}, towed vertically through a water tank at
\result[NSpeeds] speeds between \result[SpeedMin] and
\result[SpeedMax]~\si{m/s}. The resulting diameter-based Reynolds numbers
span \result[ReMin] to \result[ReMax], within the subcritical regime. Drag
was measured with a single-axis load cell, and the drag coefficient was
computed using fluid properties evaluated at the measured water temperature
of each run. Of \result[NRuns] runs, \result[NRunsExcluded] was excluded for
load cell zero drift. The mean drag coefficient over the remaining
\result[NRunsUsed] runs is \result[CdMeanAll] (standard deviation
\result[CdStdAll]), and the per-cylinder means of \result[CdMeanOneIn],
\result[CdMeanOnePointFiveIn], and \result[CdMeanTwoIn] agree within their
run-to-run scatter.
\todo[inline]{Add one sentence on the main conclusion once the blockage
correction and uncertainty analysis are settled (see Sect.~\ref{sec:discussion}).}
\end{abstract}

\listoftodos

\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

The flow past a circular cylinder is one of the canonical problems of
bluff-body aerodynamics and hydrodynamics. Over the subcritical range of
Reynolds number, roughly $10^3 \lesssim \Rey \lesssim 2\times10^5$, the
boundary layer separates laminar, the wake sheds vortices periodically, and
the drag coefficient of a nominally two-dimensional cylinder is nearly
constant~\citep{Wieselsberger1921,Roshko1961,Williamson1996,Zdravkovich1997}.
Measured values in practice also depend on blockage, aspect ratio, and end
conditions~\citep{WestApelt1982,Zdravkovich1997}.
\todo[inline]{Motivation: why these measurements? E.g., facility
commissioning, validation data for a course or for simulations, a
baseline for a later study. State the gap this paper fills and its
contribution.}

This paper describes tow tank measurements of the mean drag on three smooth
cylinders over \result[ReMin]~$\le \Rey \le$~\result[ReMax], the data
reduction used to obtain drag coefficients, and how the results compare with
the classical data.

\section{Experimental setup}
\label{sec:setup}

\subsection{Tow tank and models}

The experiments were performed in a tow tank of width
\result[TankWidth]~\si{m} and water depth \result[TankDepth]~\si{m}.
\todo[inline]{Tank length, carriage drive and speed control, speed
accuracy, acceleration profile, and wait time between runs. The run log
notes a small wave from the previous run during run 4, so the settling time
is worth stating.}
Three smooth circular cylinders of nominal diameter 1, 1.5, and 2~in
(\result[DOneIn], \result[DOnePointFiveIn], and \result[DTwoIn]~\si{mm})
were mounted vertically beneath the carriage, piercing the free surface,
with a submerged length of $L = \result[SubmergedLength]~\si{m}$. The
resulting submerged aspect ratios $L/D$ are listed in
Table~\ref{tab:summary}.
\todo[inline]{Cylinder material and surface finish; how the cylinder is
mounted to the load cell; the condition at the lower end (free end, end
plate, gap to the tank floor).}

\subsection{Force measurement}

Drag was measured with a single-axis load cell sampled at 200~\si{Hz}.
\todo[inline]{Load cell model, range, and amplifier; DAQ hardware. The DAQ
software was updated between the second and third test days, changing the
file format but not, as far as we know, the acquisition. Confirm.}
The load cell was calibrated statically by hanging known masses up to
2000~\si{g}, loading and then unloading. A linear fit of voltage against
applied force gave a sensitivity of \result[SensOld]~\si{V/N} for the first
two test days. The load cell was remounted before the third test day and
recalibrated, giving \result[SensNew]~\si{V/N}. The largest calibration fit
residual was equivalent to \result[CalResidMax]~\si{N}.

\subsection{Test matrix}

Each cylinder was tested on a single day, at \result[NSpeeds] carriage
speeds from \result[SpeedMin] to \result[SpeedMax]~\si{m/s}
(Table~\ref{tab:summary}). The water temperature was recorded for each run
and ranged from \result[TempMin] to \result[TempMax]~\si{\celsius} across
the campaign.

\section{Data reduction}
\label{sec:reduction}

Figure~\ref{fig:timeseries}(top) shows a typical force record. The carriage
was at rest for the first part of each record, accelerated to a constant
speed, towed, and decelerated back to rest. The load cell zero was taken as
the mean voltage over $\result[TareStart] \le t < \result[TareEnd]~\si{s}$,
before the carriage started moving. The mean drag $\bar{F}$ is the mean force
over the steady towing window
$\result[SteadyStart] \le t < \result[SteadyEnd]~\si{s}$. The same window was
used for every run.
\todo[inline]{Justify the window, e.g., by the number of shedding cycles it
contains and how much the mean changes when it is shortened.}
The force fluctuations visible in the steady window are due to vortex
shedding.

The drag coefficient and Reynolds number are
\begin{equation}
  C_D = \frac{\bar{F}}{\tfrac{1}{2}\rho U^2 D L}, \qquad
  \Rey = \frac{U D}{\nu},
\end{equation}
where $U$ is the carriage speed. The water density $\rho$ and kinematic
viscosity $\nu$ were evaluated at each run's measured temperature. Density
was computed from the formula of \citet{Tanaka2001} and dynamic viscosity
from the Vogel equation $\mu = A\,10^{B/(T-C)}$ with
$A = 2.414\times10^{-5}~\si{Pa.s}$, $B = 247.8~\si{K}$, and
$C = 140~\si{K}$.
\todo[inline]{Cite a source for these Vogel constants, or switch to a standard
reference correlation such as IAPWS.}
The diameter-based Froude number $\Fr = U/\sqrt{gD}$ ranged from
\result[FrMin] to \result[FrMax].

\paragraph{Run exclusion.}
The zero was also measured after each run, over
$\result[PostStart] \le t < \result[PostEnd]~\si{s}$. A run was excluded if
the zero moved by more than \result[MaxZeroDriftThreshold]~\si{N} over the
run. Only run \result[ExcludedRuns] met
this criterion: its zero shifted by \result[ExcludedZeroDrift]~\si{N}, and
its force rose steadily through the towing window
(Fig.~\ref{fig:timeseries}, bottom). The run was flagged in the run log at
the time of testing. For all retained runs the zero moved by at most
\result[MaxZeroDriftUsed]~\si{N}.
\todo[inline]{Run \result[ExcludedRuns] could be repeated if the 2~in cylinder is
tested again.}

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics{../figures/force-timeseries.pdf}
  \caption{Load cell force records. Top: a typical run. Bottom: the run
  excluded for zero drift. Shaded regions mark the windows used for the
  zero, the steady mean, and the post-run zero check.}
  \label{fig:timeseries}
\end{figure}

\section{Results}
\label{sec:results}

Figure~\ref{fig:cd} shows $C_D$ against $\Rey$ for all runs, and
Table~\ref{tab:summary} summarizes each cylinder. Across the
\result[NRunsUsed] retained runs, $C_D$ ranges from \result[CdMinAll] to
\result[CdMaxAll], with a mean of \result[CdMeanAll] and standard deviation
\result[CdStdAll]. The per-cylinder means, \result[CdMeanOneIn] for
$D = \result[DOneIn]~\si{mm}$, \result[CdMeanOnePointFiveIn] for
$D = \result[DOnePointFiveIn]~\si{mm}$, and \result[CdMeanTwoIn] for
$D = \result[DTwoIn]~\si{mm}$, differ by less than the run-to-run standard
deviation of any one cylinder. The three overlapping Reynolds number ranges
show no consistent trend common to all cylinders.
\todo[inline]{The smallest cylinder looks like it trends upward with $\Rey$, but the
others don't. Decide whether to quantify this (e.g., a fitted slope with a
confidence interval) or drop it.}
Had run \result[ExcludedRuns] been retained, its $C_D$ of
\result[CdExcluded] would have been the largest in the data set by a wide
margin.

The standard deviation of the fluctuating force in the steady window,
normalized like $C_D$, was at most \result[CdFluctMax].

\begin{figure}
  \centering
  \includegraphics{../figures/cd-vs-re.pdf}
  \caption{Drag coefficient versus Reynolds number. Fluid properties are
  evaluated at the measured water temperature. No blockage correction is
  applied.}
  \label{fig:cd}
\end{figure}

\begin{table}
